Priscilla Afia loves to research things
for the fun of knowing and the even bigger

00:02:01.668 --> 00:02:03.387
fun of sharing it with someone else.

00:02:04.007 --> 00:02:06.687
Outside of work, she's a music
lover through and through.

00:02:06.754 --> 00:02:08.983
She genuinely cannot do life without it.

00:02:09.571 --> 00:02:13.707
Reads a lot of romance and thriller
fiction, and she recently started

00:02:13.707 --> 00:02:18.539
running Chop, Chat, and Connect, a user
experience meetup in Ghana organized

00:02:18.539 --> 00:02:21.322
by her and her team members at Ecobank.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.528
Please feel free to add your questions
in the Zoom Q&A section, and we'll

00:02:26.531 --> 00:02:27.889
answer them at the end of the session.

00:02:28.561 --> 00:02:30.741
Use the chat to connect
with other attendees.

00:02:36.133 --> 00:02:37.471
Priscilla: Thank you for the introduction.

00:02:37.562 --> 00:02:41.065
It's an honor to be here to
share my presentation and

00:02:41.066 --> 00:02:42.950
also to share my experience.

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:48.125
So like mentioned before or earlier, I
am Priscilla, and I'll be speaking with

00:02:48.178 --> 00:02:53.314
you on the topic Nobody Budgets for
Accessibility Until You Make the Case.

00:02:54.402 --> 00:02:57.954
So this presentation is going to be
a blend of my personal experience

00:02:58.385 --> 00:03:03.836
and then sources from research I did
while preparing for this presentation.

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:06.656
So let's just get straight into it.

00:03:07.651 --> 00:03:09.606
So a little bit of a backstory.

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:12.371
I am Ghanaian, living in Ghana.

00:03:12.596 --> 00:03:17.128
And in Ghana, when you have completed
the university, it is required of

00:03:17.171 --> 00:03:22.115
you to serve the nation for a year,
and we like to call that the National

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.680
Service Scheme, or NSS for short.

00:03:25.265 --> 00:03:31.030
So, God being so good, um, last
year November, I was recruited as

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.353
an NSS personnel, um, at Ecobank.

00:03:34.468 --> 00:03:39.017
Um, Ecobank is a leading
Pan-African bank here in Africa.

00:03:39.542 --> 00:03:46.951
So I- I joined the UX team, that's
the user experience team, and at the

00:03:46.951 --> 00:03:51.755
start of this year, I was tasked by
my boss to conduct an accessibility

00:03:51.789 --> 00:03:54.043
audit on Ecobank's mobile app.

00:03:54.202 --> 00:03:55.466
And then not just that.

00:03:56.009 --> 00:04:00.234
After I've done with the audit, I am
supposed to present it as a business

00:04:00.236 --> 00:04:02.462
case to relevant stakeholders.

00:04:03.115 --> 00:04:08.000
And mind you, I had heard about
accessibility as a UX/UI designer,

00:04:08.424 --> 00:04:13.791
but then I didn't really truly
understand what it meant and entailed.

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:20.302
So I- I was nervous about it
because it's a big project, right?

00:04:20.714 --> 00:04:24.304
How, I, how, what am I going to tell
these relevant stakeholders, right?

00:04:24.872 --> 00:04:28.156
Um, with someone who doesn't
know much about accessibility.

00:04:29.913 --> 00:04:33.998
Um, there were so many reasons why I could
have backed out of the project, right?

00:04:34.538 --> 00:04:38.472
First being that it was a big
project, and then, um, the next

00:04:38.472 --> 00:04:43.932
reason was that accessibility is
not really practiced here in Africa.

00:04:44.206 --> 00:04:48.669
Unlike in European countries and
then in America, there are even laws

00:04:48.676 --> 00:04:50.883
that back digital accessibility.

00:04:50.885 --> 00:04:56.313
But here in Africa, and to be
specific, in my country, though we

00:04:56.318 --> 00:05:00.986
have laws, uh, backing persons with
dis- uh, disabilities, but then we

00:05:00.988 --> 00:05:06.876
do not have, um, laws backing digital
accessibility for such people.

00:05:07.681 --> 00:05:15.280
So, um, in my wrap, down my rabbit, um,
research hole, I decided to find out.

00:05:15.375 --> 00:05:20.390
This is why I decided to find
out if, um, Africa did have laws

00:05:20.442 --> 00:05:22.580
backing, um, digital accessibility.

00:05:24.207 --> 00:05:28.965
And I came across that,
okay, um, not really.

00:05:29.688 --> 00:05:35.063
So I, I was like, "Fine, I'm just gonna
go right ahead with this audit and then

00:05:35.063 --> 00:05:40.068
just get it over with," because I have
been assigned by it by my boss, right?

00:05:40.704 --> 00:05:44.515
So I did what anyone else
would have done in my shoes.

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:50.234
I started off by researching what
accessibility truly meant, and then

00:05:50.246 --> 00:05:56.203
also, um, I needed to understand
why Ecobank needed to be part of

00:05:56.203 --> 00:06:00.254
this, why Ecobank needed to conduct
an accessibility audit, and then

00:06:00.269 --> 00:06:02.669
what was in it for Ecobank, right?

00:06:04.506 --> 00:06:08.348
So, fast forward in the research
phase, this is where I discovered

00:06:08.349 --> 00:06:14.210
that there are 1.3 billion people
with disabilities globally, and this

00:06:14.211 --> 00:06:16.169
is from World Health Organization.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:21.384
And this number of people make
up 16% of the world's population.

00:06:21.829 --> 00:06:27.268
I also found out about the European
Accessibility Act, that's EAA for

00:06:27.269 --> 00:06:32.218
short, and the American Disability
Act, that's ADA for short.

00:06:33.785 --> 00:06:39.770
I was very amazed, right, to see, um,
these laws being backed, um, backing

00:06:39.838 --> 00:06:44.919
persons with disabilities outside Africa
and just like I said earlier, and there's

00:06:44.925 --> 00:06:47.050
little to none like this in Africa.

00:06:47.676 --> 00:06:55.755
And as a UX/UI designer, and with my
experience with designing with my fellow

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:01.373
designers and then even developers,
we never really take, um, digital

00:07:01.376 --> 00:07:03.247
accessibility into consideration.

00:07:03.254 --> 00:07:08.030
We just go ahead, design, and
then hand over to the developers

00:07:08.054 --> 00:07:11.484
and then they go ahead, bring it
live, and then it's out there.

00:07:11.934 --> 00:07:17.214
Um, you barely hear of, um,
designers here in Africa talk

00:07:17.222 --> 00:07:18.888
about digital accessibility.

00:07:20.336 --> 00:07:26.324
So, um, in the same research phase,
I moved on to find out if, um, Africa

00:07:26.776 --> 00:07:33.047
was, um, having laws backing persons
with disabilities and if we really

00:07:33.048 --> 00:07:35.005
did practice digital accessibility.

00:07:35.323 --> 00:07:40.906
And then I found out that, um, according
to the UN statistics, there are 80

00:07:40.921 --> 00:07:47.513
million persons with disability living in
Africa, and this is where Ecobank thrives.

00:07:47.884 --> 00:07:55.599
There are 34 Ecobank branches, if I
should say, in, um, in Ecobank, right?

00:07:56.241 --> 00:07:56.894
Okay, maybe.

00:07:57.391 --> 00:08:02.607
So there are 34… Ecobank is
represented in 34 different countries.

00:08:03.863 --> 00:08:04.144
Right.

00:08:04.606 --> 00:08:09.845
And then these 80 million people
with disabilities, that's a whole set

00:08:09.887 --> 00:08:12.224
of customer range we are ignoring.

00:08:13.014 --> 00:08:18.034
So I was, I was beginning to understand
that, okay, then the need for this

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:24.660
ac- um, audit is really, really
necessary because we may be ignoring

00:08:24.696 --> 00:08:29.513
this, these set of customers, which
are worth 30 million people, right?

00:08:32.265 --> 00:08:35.781
Um, still in the rabbit, um,
research rabbit hole, I got to

00:08:35.781 --> 00:08:40.704
understand the various benefits a
company enjoys when they adopt the

00:08:40.705 --> 00:08:42.413
principle of accessible design.

00:08:42.892 --> 00:08:46.422
So I got to find out that some
benefits are that there is more

00:08:46.422 --> 00:08:48.193
traffic on your digital product.

00:08:48.644 --> 00:08:53.100
Um, more traffic here I mean that,
um, you have more and more people.

00:08:53.102 --> 00:08:58.184
So imagine 80 million people, 80
million new people, um, getting on

00:08:58.185 --> 00:09:02.444
board on your digital app or getting
on board on Ecobank's mobile app.

00:09:02.884 --> 00:09:04.223
That's like more traffic.

00:09:04.543 --> 00:09:08.637
And then the more the traffic, the more
the chance the company stands in winning

00:09:08.638 --> 00:09:10.921
more money or getting more money, right?

00:09:11.237 --> 00:09:18.702
So another benefit is also, um,
reduced lawsuits for countries

00:09:18.713 --> 00:09:22.963
that have, um, legal laws backing,
um, digital accessibility.

00:09:23.401 --> 00:09:28.972
And then also it's, there's the
need for better engine, search

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:30.986
engine optimization, right?

00:09:31.502 --> 00:09:33.829
And then also not forgetting
your brand perception.

00:09:33.837 --> 00:09:39.991
So if, um, people are able to
identify your company as, oh, they

00:09:39.994 --> 00:09:42.104
are practicing inclusiveness, right?

00:09:42.588 --> 00:09:47.641
Um- It gives better perception
to your customers, not just your

00:09:47.657 --> 00:09:53.070
customers, like others other than your
customers, right, about your company.

00:09:53.512 --> 00:09:59.253
So most—I also realized that most
companies fail to adopt this principle

00:09:59.254 --> 00:10:06.275
of, um, access- accessible design, and
I think this would be because they don't

00:10:06.550 --> 00:10:13.625
really and truly understand yet the
benefits in terms of profit, in terms of

00:10:13.625 --> 00:10:20.141
revenue, that accessibility could bring or
give to their, um, respective companies.

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:20.855
Right.

00:10:21.265 --> 00:10:24.461
So at this point, I was very
much convinced that, okay, I

00:10:24.461 --> 00:10:26.532
am ready to conduct the audits.

00:10:26.554 --> 00:10:29.657
I am ready to go down the
hill and conduct the audits.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:34.223
Now, in order to start the
audit, I needed to plan.

00:10:35.127 --> 00:10:35.405
Right.

00:10:35.429 --> 00:10:40.947
I needed to plan the audit, and before
I started with the planning itself,

00:10:41.271 --> 00:10:46.077
I had to prepare documents to, um,
the stakeholders I was going to make

00:10:46.077 --> 00:10:48.358
a business case for, um, sorry, to.

00:10:48.932 --> 00:10:55.540
So, um, these stakeholders included the
group head, who was the chief digital

00:10:55.607 --> 00:10:59.271
officer, and then also the head of
digital business channel management.

00:10:59.825 --> 00:11:05.297
So this meeting was to just
prepare them f- um, for what

00:11:05.319 --> 00:11:06.912
was ahead, for what is ahead.

00:11:07.286 --> 00:11:07.703
Right.

00:11:08.067 --> 00:11:12.589
So, um, I believe I did this because I
needed them to be a part of the journey

00:11:13.562 --> 00:11:18.838
right from the start, so it doesn't
become stressful and more difficult to

00:11:18.838 --> 00:11:23.811
get them at the end of the phase or the
whole process, just to get them to hear

00:11:23.812 --> 00:11:25.698
what I have to say about accessibility.

00:11:26.398 --> 00:11:26.697
Right.

00:11:27.944 --> 00:11:34.560
So, um, I, I made a, there were several
meetings that were held where I presented

00:11:34.566 --> 00:11:40.812
my findings on what I had learnt about,
um, accessibility as a whole, um, what

00:11:40.825 --> 00:11:47.997
accessibility meant, the benefits of
digital accessibility, um, the legal

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:52.595
laws, countries that practice, um, digital
accessibility and the laws that were

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:55.010
backing, um, this digital accessibility.

00:11:55.791 --> 00:11:58.050
Um, sorry, this is digital accessibility.

00:11:59.567 --> 00:12:07.037
So, um, they were—I guess they
were much more enthusiasts about

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:09.561
it, like the way I was, right?

00:12:10.005 --> 00:12:16.808
It's, it got them, it got them
to really understand, um, what

00:12:16.934 --> 00:12:19.204
digital accessibility truly means.

00:12:19.263 --> 00:12:23.739
It got them to prepare their minds for
the audit that I was about to make.

00:12:24.091 --> 00:12:28.702
Um, and then they gave me the
go-ahead to go ahead, conduct the

00:12:28.703 --> 00:12:34.191
audit, and then we, we discussed
the next steps after the audit.

00:12:35.465 --> 00:12:38.399
Okay, so this is where I actually
start the planning of the audit.

00:12:38.953 --> 00:12:45.531
Uh, so in the planning stage, I decide
to go… I decided to go with the WCAG 2.2

00:12:45.865 --> 00:12:52.721
level AA as a criteria, while I adopted
the POUR principles, which is perceivable,

00:12:53.144 --> 00:12:58.307
operable, understandable, and robust,
um, principles or as the framework.

00:12:59.475 --> 00:13:05.418
So I discovered this, um, criteria and
then this pr- POUR principle from the

00:13:05.420 --> 00:13:10.858
research I did earlier, um, when preparing
to meet with the stakeholders just to

00:13:10.858 --> 00:13:12.579
prepare their minds for what was ahead.

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:20.992
And, um, I plan to use the Accessibility
Scanner as an automated tool.

00:13:21.513 --> 00:13:25.146
Uh, so, um, Accessibility
Scanner is a free Android app

00:13:25.411 --> 00:13:27.516
by Google that helps developers-

00:13:29.726 --> 00:13:34.769
And designers identify accessibility
issues in their apps, and it also

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:36.730
provides actionable recommendations.

00:13:37.386 --> 00:13:44.714
So in the, um, auditing phase, um, there
were some issues that were detected.

00:13:45.093 --> 00:13:49.915
And some issues were some text were
too faint against background to read.

00:13:50.342 --> 00:13:52.680
Some buttons were too
small to tap accurately.

00:13:53.647 --> 00:13:58.062
A user with limited hand control
could easily miss a tap on

00:13:58.074 --> 00:14:00.330
such but- um, buttons, right?

00:14:00.904 --> 00:14:03.856
Or if they were to attempt
to tap them wrongly.

00:14:04.952 --> 00:14:05.202
Right.

00:14:05.709 --> 00:14:09.425
So I documented all these
results, provided some actionable

00:14:09.438 --> 00:14:14.838
fixes and implementation, not
forgetting the value and the ask.

00:14:15.334 --> 00:14:20.147
Because that's what you are conducting
this accessibility for, um, audit for.

00:14:21.051 --> 00:14:21.763
It's for the ask.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:22.720
It's mainly for the ask.

00:14:23.461 --> 00:14:29.875
And so the value here is, um, the
exposure of Ecobank to a wider set

00:14:29.886 --> 00:14:34.961
of customers, which would boost the
company's, um, revenue or profit, and

00:14:34.993 --> 00:14:40.300
also Ecobank being perceived as an
inclusive customer-centric African bank.

00:14:40.799 --> 00:14:46.398
And for what our customers were going
to gain from this accessibility as

00:14:46.438 --> 00:14:51.322
a value, accessibility audit as a
value, is what… Is that they were

00:14:51.335 --> 00:14:57.518
going to experience, um, enhanced
experience for all customers, including

00:14:57.585 --> 00:14:59.017
persons with disabilities, right?

00:14:59.789 --> 00:15:03.447
So they were going to get better
experiences, especially persons

00:15:03.451 --> 00:15:08.929
with disabilities, um, engaging
with, um, Ecobank's mobile app.

00:15:09.697 --> 00:15:17.865
And for the ask, it was mainly to
rant about what accessibility is,

00:15:18.531 --> 00:15:25.560
and then, um, provide them with, um,
the list of issues and then also,

00:15:25.618 --> 00:15:29.888
um, the actionable, um, next steps.

00:15:30.275 --> 00:15:34.698
And after the value has been
communicated as well, um, the final

00:15:34.698 --> 00:15:38.675
step is the ask, which is to ask
for a budget for accessibility.

00:15:39.798 --> 00:15:40.172
Right.

00:15:40.581 --> 00:15:42.065
So I reached out to Deque.

00:15:42.362 --> 00:15:46.610
Deque is a leading accessibility
consultant, and they ran us through

00:15:46.965 --> 00:15:51.324
several or numerous, uh, meetings
where they shared with not just

00:15:51.349 --> 00:15:55.570
my team, but then with the quality
assurance team, with the developers

00:15:55.603 --> 00:15:59.498
and other relevant stakeholders, and
more of their expertise in the field

00:15:59.946 --> 00:16:05.840
and how their various tools, their
various Axe tools could help Ecobank.

00:16:10.269 --> 00:16:12.354
Okay, so what did change?

00:16:12.841 --> 00:16:21.574
Um, so while we-- I had gone ahead,
conducted the accessibility audit, it

00:16:21.577 --> 00:16:23.736
was now the time to present my findings.

00:16:24.125 --> 00:16:27.797
And so I went ahead, presented my
findings, and then they did like the

00:16:27.799 --> 00:16:32.820
idea of digital accessibility, and they
hadn't really, like, paid much attention

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:36.134
to it or they were delighted, right?

00:16:36.136 --> 00:16:41.172
They were delighted to have, um,
gotten this feedback from the audit

00:16:41.234 --> 00:16:44.143
that was, um, gotten or conducted.

00:16:44.769 --> 00:16:50.312
So, um, what changed actually was
that accessibility became a compulsory

00:16:50.318 --> 00:16:55.230
course, um, for everyone here at
Ecobank to take on, to understand

00:16:55.259 --> 00:16:58.157
truly in depth what accessibility was.

00:16:58.748 --> 00:17:08.089
And also it was to make, um, to reach
a wider range of teams in the company

00:17:08.141 --> 00:17:10.074
to understand what accessibility is.

00:17:10.675 --> 00:17:15.768
And then also, um, a budget was
drawn or is yet to be drawn.

00:17:16.183 --> 00:17:21.381
Yes, a budget is yet to be drawn because,
um, as at the time the audit was done

00:17:21.444 --> 00:17:25.770
and then I presented the findings, the
budget for the year had already been

00:17:25.781 --> 00:17:31.705
drawn, but then accessibility has been
taken into consideration for, um, 2027.

00:17:32.588 --> 00:17:32.805
Right.

00:17:33.322 --> 00:17:38.375
And then also, um, another thing
that changed was, um, we got,

00:17:38.720 --> 00:17:41.193
um, the most important teams.

00:17:41.234 --> 00:17:47.615
That's the developers, our quality
assurance team, and also other relevant

00:17:47.622 --> 00:17:54.855
stakeholders to truly understand what,
um, digital accessibility is and how

00:17:54.885 --> 00:17:58.863
Ecobank was going to, is going to
benefit from digital accessibility

00:17:59.457 --> 00:18:03.635
or making our digital product
accessible to persons with disability.

00:18:03.644 --> 00:18:08.273
And all this was done and gotten
from Deque while they were

00:18:08.273 --> 00:18:09.398
running us through their sessions.

00:18:11.029 --> 00:18:15.792
So now what has the audit taught me about
making a accessibility a business case?

00:18:16.222 --> 00:18:23.234
If you were to ask me again to conduct
an accessibility all over again, um, for

00:18:23.270 --> 00:18:28.062
the past or for the future and, and to
make a business case out of it for the

00:18:28.067 --> 00:18:34.505
company I work with, I would say firstly,
findings alone do not move the people you

00:18:34.505 --> 00:18:37.309
are going to be doing the ask for, right?

00:18:37.322 --> 00:18:38.445
So what does this mean?

00:18:39.456 --> 00:18:48.388
So, um, instead of just listing out
a checklist of, we found this, um,

00:18:48.401 --> 00:18:53.225
these are the issues we found, and
then you are just going to, um, present

00:18:53.226 --> 00:18:59.691
it to the stakeholders, um, they are
not really going to be moved by that.

00:19:00.320 --> 00:19:03.026
And so a little bit of a story.

00:19:03.027 --> 00:19:07.758
So I remember when I was in the
research phase, there was a whole lot

00:19:07.761 --> 00:19:09.306
I was learning about accessibility.

00:19:09.654 --> 00:19:15.398
What accessibility is, um, the
various laws that were backing, um,

00:19:15.432 --> 00:19:18.499
the European countries and America.

00:19:18.782 --> 00:19:21.284
I was learning a whole lot
about digital accessibilities,

00:19:21.305 --> 00:19:22.965
and I felt so excited about it.

00:19:23.928 --> 00:19:25.643
Felt so super excited about it.

00:19:26.174 --> 00:19:31.089
And I felt that, okay, these people
I am going to be making the business

00:19:31.111 --> 00:19:35.093
case from, they are going to feel the
same way I'm feeling right now with

00:19:35.121 --> 00:19:37.393
all this information I have, right?

00:19:37.425 --> 00:19:41.130
So I was done with the research phase.

00:19:41.156 --> 00:19:47.634
I drafted the report, and then I met
with my boss and I was presenting

00:19:47.650 --> 00:19:52.577
to him just to prep me for the final
meeting day with the, the stakeholders.

00:19:53.024 --> 00:20:00.155
And after I was done with my preparations,
my presentation, he was like, "Okay, I

00:20:00.156 --> 00:20:01.570
like the fact that you are learning a lot.

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:05.138
I like that you are so excited
about what you are learning." He

00:20:05.139 --> 00:20:10.740
even made me present to some people
about, uh, what I had learnt, right?

00:20:11.313 --> 00:20:15.000
But then he told me that, um,
these findings alone do not

00:20:15.066 --> 00:20:17.488
move the stakeholders, right?

00:20:17.885 --> 00:20:24.215
So what he meant here was that, um, yes,
it's okay to present the findings, right?

00:20:24.732 --> 00:20:28.151
The screenshots of the, where
the issues are, what the

00:20:28.173 --> 00:20:29.749
issues themselves are, right?

00:20:30.199 --> 00:20:36.655
But then you should do that while
communicating value, while communicating

00:20:36.658 --> 00:20:41.777
the value and then speak in numbers
because these people speak in numbers.

00:20:41.827 --> 00:20:43.313
That's their language, right?

00:20:44.955 --> 00:20:49.062
So, um, I was like, "Okay, I
have to go back and then really

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:50.457
think this through." Right?

00:20:50.476 --> 00:20:56.061
So if I was or I were to go back to
conduct the whole audit again or in

00:20:56.061 --> 00:21:01.888
my future, um, auditing of digital
accessibility on digital products, I

00:21:01.888 --> 00:21:08.485
would say that when you are making a
business case for this especially, you

00:21:08.487 --> 00:21:16.927
should not, um, just list out the issues
and then hope that they would understand

00:21:16.929 --> 00:21:18.159
what you are talking about, right?

00:21:18.668 --> 00:21:22.543
But then you should be able to,
um, communicate in their language.

00:21:22.785 --> 00:21:25.091
That is, pick up the numbers.

00:21:25.392 --> 00:21:29.185
Um, so how are other
companies doing this, right?

00:21:29.246 --> 00:21:30.664
How are other companies doing this?

00:21:30.686 --> 00:21:34.641
How are, how are the other companies
benefiting from this opportunity?

00:21:34.926 --> 00:21:35.159
Right.

00:21:35.178 --> 00:21:39.492
How are they benefiting from
investing in digital accessibility?

00:21:39.921 --> 00:21:42.676
Um, how is their return on investment?

00:21:43.087 --> 00:21:48.472
Um, you should be able to bring
out numbers from this to, to,

00:21:48.472 --> 00:21:49.949
to get them on board, right?

00:21:50.044 --> 00:21:54.040
To move them that, okay, if company--
other companies are doing this and then

00:21:54.063 --> 00:21:59.692
they are really reaping a whole lot,
then We would, we would do the same.

00:22:00.471 --> 00:22:00.846
Right.

00:22:01.206 --> 00:22:05.890
So, uh, while you've communicated the
value, you've communicated, um, the

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:14.069
numbers, um, keep making sure you are
using the language the stakeholders speak.

00:22:14.314 --> 00:22:16.455
So what are some languages
the stakeholders speak?

00:22:16.456 --> 00:22:22.755
So as a UX, UX/UI designer, um, one
of the most important flows I have

00:22:22.776 --> 00:22:29.401
come to discover is that, um, is the
onboarding flow or the onboarding process.

00:22:29.416 --> 00:22:34.661
So why do I say the onboarding process or
flow of a digital product is important?

00:22:34.675 --> 00:22:39.976
So it's quite important because--
It's very important, not quite.

00:22:40.071 --> 00:22:46.455
It's very important because, um, this
is where your users are trying to get

00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:51.175
into your digital product to buy your
services and to buy your products from.

00:22:51.639 --> 00:22:57.548
So you should try as much as possible
to make that flow, make that screen,

00:22:57.559 --> 00:23:04.954
make that process zero frustrations, no
frustrations at all, and remove every

00:23:04.954 --> 00:23:11.614
friction because your users, um, they are
making the decision, "Okay, I get here, I

00:23:11.616 --> 00:23:17.216
am entering my details, and then I click
on the button, and I go into the app, and

00:23:17.216 --> 00:23:18.937
then I go and make the purchases," right?

00:23:19.819 --> 00:23:24.401
So, um, these are key areas stakeholders
pay particular attention to.

00:23:24.830 --> 00:23:29.226
And then under these key areas, they
pay particular attention to especially

00:23:29.241 --> 00:23:30.885
drop-offs—keywords like drop-offs.

00:23:31.337 --> 00:23:36.680
So, an example: um, assume I am
making—I'm presenting this business

00:23:36.681 --> 00:23:39.026
case to you as a stakeholder, right?

00:23:39.043 --> 00:23:44.745
So I would say that, um, during the audit,
I found out that in the onboarding phase

00:23:45.169 --> 00:23:51.003
or in our onboarding screen or on our
onboarding screen, um, I found out that,

00:23:51.764 --> 00:23:59.257
um, these customers, our customers are
able to go through- By inputting, um,

00:23:59.637 --> 00:24:04.425
their details that is required from them,
the details that is required from them.

00:24:04.833 --> 00:24:10.963
But then when they get to clicking
the button, um, a screen reader, a

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:16.561
particular user who uses a screen reader
is not able to move on because the

00:24:16.562 --> 00:24:22.240
screen reader is not able to spell out,
um, what they are tapping on, right?

00:24:22.265 --> 00:24:25.971
So that is a friction there,
and that causes a drop-off.

00:24:25.984 --> 00:24:31.532
So they will just drop off and then go
find another app to buy from, right?

00:24:31.561 --> 00:24:34.551
Or a fellow competitor's
app to use, right?

00:24:34.557 --> 00:24:40.018
So pay particular attention to the
keywords and stakeholder likes, um,

00:24:40.074 --> 00:24:43.469
to hear, if I should say, for lack
of a better word, like to hear.

00:24:43.971 --> 00:24:48.731
Um, also, while you are communicating
all this, make sure you are

00:24:48.738 --> 00:24:50.377
doing it in a storytelling form.

00:24:51.611 --> 00:24:58.120
Um, don't just write a report and then
you are going from, "Okay, we mentioned

00:24:58.184 --> 00:25:00.872
that we saw that there's this, there's
that, there's this, there's that."

00:25:01.275 --> 00:25:01.553
Okay.

00:25:01.784 --> 00:25:09.987
You should be able to put the stakeholders
in the shoes of these people, right?

00:25:10.007 --> 00:25:15.960
How would a person with disability,
um, feel when they are going through

00:25:15.988 --> 00:25:21.625
our various flows or screens in our
mobile app or on our mobile app?

00:25:21.922 --> 00:25:24.095
How are they—What are the feelings?

00:25:24.368 --> 00:25:25.518
What are the frictions?

00:25:25.862 --> 00:25:30.403
You should be able to put this, these
stakeholders in the shoes of these people,

00:25:31.558 --> 00:25:37.006
um, so they're able to truly feel what
they are, um, these people are feeling

00:25:37.407 --> 00:25:42.070
or what the customers are feeling, and
then they get a better sense of, "Okay,

00:25:42.419 --> 00:25:44.326
we really need to rectify this issue."

00:25:44.917 --> 00:25:48.309
And so as you are going ahead with
the storytelling, you are also

00:25:48.309 --> 00:25:49.809
telling them about the impact.

00:25:49.890 --> 00:25:52.737
What is in it for Ecobank
as you are spelling it out.

00:25:53.441 --> 00:25:57.899
So if you've communicated the value,
you've communicated the key, you've

00:25:57.901 --> 00:26:07.371
used keywords, um, another thing I
would, um, recommend a lot is using

00:26:07.402 --> 00:26:12.213
real-world examples when you are
making your business case, right?

00:26:12.686 --> 00:26:21.287
So, um, these relevant stakeholders,
they would want to know how truly is

00:26:21.296 --> 00:26:27.431
this being, uh, how truly is this digital
accessibility benefiting other companies?

00:26:28.216 --> 00:26:29.040
I want to know.

00:26:29.092 --> 00:26:31.797
Show me: what are other companies doing?

00:26:32.113 --> 00:26:36.792
You need to be able to benchmark
with other companies, right?

00:26:37.249 --> 00:26:39.162
What's, what are they gaining from it?

00:26:39.199 --> 00:26:40.791
Is it shooting up their profits?

00:26:40.815 --> 00:26:41.696
Is it what?

00:26:43.039 --> 00:26:43.999
You get it, right?

00:26:44.050 --> 00:26:44.323
Okay.

00:26:44.644 --> 00:26:50.311
So, um- There are some example,
there are a whole lot of examples

00:26:50.619 --> 00:26:56.202
of, um, how digital accessibility
is, um, um, companies are benefiting

00:26:56.215 --> 00:26:58.495
from digital accessibility, right?

00:26:58.539 --> 00:26:58.706
So

00:27:00.731 --> 00:27:04.597
Another world example of how digital
accessibility is impacting the

00:27:04.945 --> 00:27:12.759
return on investment of companies,
um, is that you would, um…

00:27:13.988 --> 00:27:20.464
So in the example you are setting, again,
do not forget to communicate the value,

00:27:20.768 --> 00:27:24.143
the numbers, right, and then the examples.

00:27:24.436 --> 00:27:27.803
So an example of the values
you can communicate here.

00:27:28.092 --> 00:27:36.241
Sorry, an example of the numbers you can
communicate here is, um, the combined

00:27:36.258 --> 00:27:44.416
spending power of people with disabilities
and their family globally is $13 trillion.

00:27:46.350 --> 00:27:48.635
Yes, you heard that right: $13 trillion.

00:27:49.025 --> 00:27:53.199
If you had to combine the spending
power of people with disabilities and

00:27:53.202 --> 00:28:00.984
their family globally, that makes up
13 billion, um, $13 trillion, right?

00:28:01.151 --> 00:28:03.533
And this isn't just a niche market.

00:28:04.032 --> 00:28:11.840
It's a market leadership everyone
is, um, already trying to capture and

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:13.439
probably they just don't know that…

00:28:14.270 --> 00:28:17.327
They don't, they just don't know
it in the accessibility sense yet.

00:28:17.924 --> 00:28:22.091
Okay, so another, um, example
you could use, um, or you could

00:28:22.118 --> 00:28:27.532
communicate with to your stakeholder
is, um, the example of Tesco.

00:28:27.897 --> 00:28:33.918
So Tesco is a British multinational
grocery and general merchandise retailer.

00:28:34.483 --> 00:28:42.614
So in 2001, they spent roughly £35,000 on
accessibility improvements to their site.

00:28:43.087 --> 00:28:47.998
And this investment they made
unlocked thirty million pounds

00:28:48.285 --> 00:28:50.209
in revenue just in a single year.

00:28:51.791 --> 00:28:56.679
So you can tell that this landmark, um,
case study demonstrated significant,

00:28:57.126 --> 00:29:04.942
um, financial impact of inclusive design
and on user engagement and conversions.

00:29:06.395 --> 00:29:12.302
And lastly, an example you can
use is the Forrester's research.

00:29:12.501 --> 00:29:17.814
So according to Forrester, if you
indicate… Sorry, according to

00:29:17.819 --> 00:29:25.069
Forrester, um, if you invest one
dollar or if you spend one dollar

00:29:25.495 --> 00:29:29.164
on UX, it returns, um, $100.

00:29:29.932 --> 00:29:37.129
So with accessibility being under UX,
um, we can see that if you spend $1

00:29:37.503 --> 00:29:44.585
on accessibility, that means you are
investing in accessibility, and that can

00:29:44.588 --> 00:29:47.759
yield up to about 100,000% of returns.

00:29:48.707 --> 00:29:53.814
So these are a list of examples you
can use, um, research on from Forrester

00:29:53.836 --> 00:30:03.449
on- The 1% and $1 spent spending
that yields $100 back as return, and

00:30:03.452 --> 00:30:09.542
then how, um, Tesco benefited from
investing in accessibility, um, digital

00:30:09.542 --> 00:30:11.910
accessibility on their websites.

00:30:12.233 --> 00:30:17.205
And then you can think also about the
spending power, the combined spending

00:30:17.209 --> 00:30:21.779
power of persons with disability and
their family, which makes up $13 trillion

00:30:24.475 --> 00:30:24.844
Okay.

00:30:25.213 --> 00:30:31.425
So to round up, um, all my learnings
and my experiences, there are some tips

00:30:31.427 --> 00:30:36.483
that I would leave for you or with you
when you are making a business case.

00:30:36.751 --> 00:30:41.288
And yes, I would highly recommend that
even if you are not thinking about it,

00:30:41.334 --> 00:30:47.696
you should start thinking about preparing
and yourself to make a business case for

00:30:47.697 --> 00:30:53.267
digital accessibility, because I believe
every company, not just companies, every

00:30:53.271 --> 00:30:57.866
company, every business should be able to,
um, think digital accessibility because,

00:30:58.199 --> 00:31:06.042
um, we are, you are really missing out
on, um, the 16% of the population, um,

00:31:06.314 --> 00:31:09.684
that could be your new market, right?

00:31:10.576 --> 00:31:13.261
Which in turn boosts your revenue.

00:31:14.739 --> 00:31:21.551
So one tip I could, um- Leave
you with is you need to be able

00:31:21.551 --> 00:31:23.172
to lead with something tangible.

00:31:24.337 --> 00:31:30.427
So, um, when you are starting the audit,
you need to first of all prepare your

00:31:30.439 --> 00:31:32.557
stakeholders and your team, right?

00:31:33.298 --> 00:31:34.767
Have them prepared.

00:31:35.057 --> 00:31:39.210
Don't just go ahead, conduct the
thing, the audit, and then just

00:31:39.842 --> 00:31:43.971
come slam it in their face and then
expect them to give you the ask.

00:31:43.971 --> 00:31:49.672
I mean, it, it may work, but if you really
want it to be like a fast achievement,

00:31:50.085 --> 00:31:54.895
but a fast achievement could I would
say that, um, it should involve them.

00:31:55.745 --> 00:32:02.760
And then you don't necessarily have
to, um, make an ask for conducting the

00:32:02.812 --> 00:32:05.117
audit in order to present it to them.

00:32:05.471 --> 00:32:10.085
So, uh, like I mentioned earlier,
when I was conducting the audit,

00:32:10.136 --> 00:32:15.525
I used, um, Accessibility Scanner
as a, as an automated tool, right?

00:32:15.584 --> 00:32:17.784
There are several other
automated tools you could use.

00:32:18.099 --> 00:32:23.135
Accessibility Scanner for your Android
apps, and you can also use WAVE.

00:32:23.507 --> 00:32:27.808
WAVE is for, um , is a checklist.

00:32:27.832 --> 00:32:32.064
It's a checking tool for
websites especially, right?

00:32:32.292 --> 00:32:34.394
So there are a lot of
automated tools you could use.

00:32:34.661 --> 00:32:39.894
So, um, I would say that you
should start with prepping them and

00:32:39.897 --> 00:32:47.382
then in your audit, um, phase or
process, f- get free tools, right?

00:32:47.432 --> 00:32:48.075
Get free tools.

00:32:48.396 --> 00:32:54.513
They would… Even though these free
tools do not, um, they do not capture

00:32:54.642 --> 00:32:58.851
100 of all the issues because yes,
you need automated tools, but then

00:32:58.853 --> 00:33:03.113
you would also need to test with, um,
you would also need to test manually

00:33:03.378 --> 00:33:05.278
with persons with disability, right?

00:33:05.300 --> 00:33:08.638
So you can't just depend
on 100% on automated tools.

00:33:09.079 --> 00:33:16.850
But then as the start, just to get, um,
your stakeholders in, um, in order to get

00:33:16.890 --> 00:33:20.206
them to, um, bring out the budgets, right?

00:33:20.622 --> 00:33:26.117
Um, I would say that you get started
with free automated tools, and these

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:29.160
free automated tools are enough for
you to make your business case, right?

00:33:29.560 --> 00:33:31.039
And also

00:33:33.124 --> 00:33:38.796
Um, I've already mentioned
some, um, automated tools like

00:33:38.798 --> 00:33:40.098
the accessibility scanner.

00:33:40.369 --> 00:33:44.127
So how that works is that after
you have downloaded it and you've

00:33:44.138 --> 00:33:48.499
activated it, there's a floating blue
button where it takes screenshots

00:33:48.518 --> 00:33:50.846
or screen records of multiple pages.

00:33:50.847 --> 00:33:56.156
So a screenshot if it's a single
page, and you can take a screen record

00:33:56.171 --> 00:34:02.172
and it's able to capture, um, um,
issues on multiple screens, right?

00:34:02.699 --> 00:34:09.221
So yeah, after you've taken the screen
records or you've taken the snapshots,

00:34:09.608 --> 00:34:15.716
um, it's also able to give you, um,
a whole documentation that speaks in

00:34:15.717 --> 00:34:24.042
depth into the, that speaks in depth
into the, um, the issue at hand.

00:34:24.293 --> 00:34:27.702
So it's gonna list out the issues on
the screen, and then when you tap on

00:34:27.721 --> 00:34:32.656
them, it's going to tell you just a
summary of it, and then it's gonna give

00:34:32.661 --> 00:34:37.900
you a whole documentation that explains
into it, um, what the issues are.

00:34:38.975 --> 00:34:41.815
And then another tip I would say
is to remember to speak in the

00:34:41.816 --> 00:34:43.896
language of the stakeholder, right?

00:34:43.925 --> 00:34:45.907
So remember to use stakeholder languages.

00:34:46.300 --> 00:34:50.854
Um, example, the keywords such as
conversions, return on investment,

00:34:50.872 --> 00:34:55.684
retentions, uh, not just going
about accessibility as a keyword.

00:34:56.619 --> 00:34:56.850
Okay.

00:34:57.229 --> 00:35:00.679
And also it's crucial to benchmark
how accessibility is greatly

00:35:00.679 --> 00:35:02.649
impacting other companies.

00:35:02.666 --> 00:35:07.790
So you have to do your research on,
on these areas, how it's, um, are

00:35:07.841 --> 00:35:12.741
benefiting other companies, and then
you, um, communicate that, okay,

00:35:12.759 --> 00:35:14.674
and not forgetting the numbers.

00:35:15.557 --> 00:35:15.919
Okay.

00:35:15.968 --> 00:35:19.799
And then also lastly, the last tip I would
say is you do not have to do it alone.

00:35:20.688 --> 00:35:23.725
I highly believe that
accessibility has to be…

00:35:24.011 --> 00:35:25.288
Everyone has to be involved.

00:35:25.598 --> 00:35:28.788
So get the relevant team on board,
your developers, your quality

00:35:28.788 --> 00:35:32.319
assurance team, project managers,
product, uh, product managers.

00:35:32.391 --> 00:35:36.984
Get everyone on board,
and it'll be a great start

00:35:42.469 --> 00:35:42.935
Okay.

00:35:43.321 --> 00:35:44.114
So,

00:35:46.168 --> 00:35:52.629
I left out here some links to, um, having
access to the tools I have recommended.

00:35:52.982 --> 00:35:56.444
That's Axe, and then also there's a
video here that takes you through,

00:35:56.475 --> 00:35:59.005
um, how to use Accessibility Scanner.

00:35:59.315 --> 00:36:03.633
And also, um, there are some articles here
you can read, further read on how to make

00:36:03.634 --> 00:36:05.243
a business case for digital accessibility.

